Base: 93f51579e7df248780214094418f205253383cc5 (Linux 7.3-rc4).

Allocation and compatibility
----------------------------

Every ring allocation uses the chunk allocator. Ordinary guests and
buffers kept private by their channel policy use vzalloc(). When a buffer
requires host-shared pages in an encrypted or isolated guest, it uses
contiguous chunks, transitions each through page_address(), then combines
them with vmap(). Order descent includes order zero. Guest-memory
encryption is selected independently of Hyper-V isolation from patch 0001.
All eight isolation/encryption/confidentiality combinations execute in
the final decision test; this does not qualify confidential hardware.

Ring consumers select co_ring_buffer. External buffers and the legacy
allocator select co_external_memory. The exported allocator and
caller-decrypted GPADL prototypes retain four arguments through all
thirteen stages. Private entry points carry the additional ownership
and policy state. UIO's allocation migration follows its release support.

The address, page/chunk arrays and embedded GPADL belong to one buffer.
Allocation extent, host-described GPADL extent and retained allocation
extent remain distinct. Interior GPADL duplication supports the existing
exported interfaces. Prepared owned buffers avoid repeat decryption;
HV_GPADL_BUFFER_DECRYPTED is removed.

Lifetime and host acknowledgment
--------------------------------

A host rescind alone does not prove GPADL revocation. For a known-live
GPADL with its channel identifier still held, teardown posts a request
and requires a matching GPADL_TORNDOWN message before clearing ownership.
Synthetic rescind notification cannot substitute for this acknowledgment.
Unknown creates, local removal, invalid identifiers, failed posting and
timeouts retain ownership. Waiter removal shares the response-list lock
with matching replies so a late acknowledgment cannot use a freed waiter.

Released owners also retain pages across mapping references and failed
page-state restoration. Reclaimer shutdown cancels pending timers while
holding the owner lock and requeues only work actually canceled. Already
running callbacks drain through workqueue destruction without a second
execution of their possibly freed embedded work item.

Sysfs mappings install no vm_ops, as kernfs requires, and release the
bridge pin on every setup outcome. UIO mappings hold their pin through
VMA close. Page/count/protection snapshots share the owner-lock boundary.
Two new tests call the real sysfs wrapper, insert actual PTEs and check
readable bytes and page references through success and partial -EBUSY.
They do not execute a full kernfs syscall or full device destruction.

The final three fixes use kvcalloc() for requestors, kvzalloc_obj() for
guest-private RNDIS descriptors, and accept NULL control requests for
empty netvsc completions. Their Fixes objects are independently pinned.
They can be routed separately through the netvsc maintainers.

Measured software and ordinary Windows evidence
-----------------------------------------------

The exact final tree passes 68 linked x86_64 QEMU KUnit cases: 33 VMBus,
2 actual sysfs/MM cases, 14 UIO, 11 netvsc completion, 4 RNDIS and
4 refcount cases. Reintroducing the shutdown requeue decision fails two
real-workqueue running-window cases. Removing production wrapper unpin
fails both actual-MM cases. Restoring the source passes all 68 again.
Complete VMBus unload and retained-owner destruction remain unqualified.

A distinct WSL backport passes 25 production-request/matcher cases.
Its module-enabled kernel and paired module image boot on actual Windows:
64 ordinary commands produce 456 matching host teardown acknowledgments,
zero close errors and zero final-batch physical-backing growth. All 751
module-tree files match and actual module loading succeeds. The exact
pair is installed locally; current identity/log and short passive
observations pass. This qualifies that backport's measured ordinary
behavior, not confidential transitions or these mainline mail bytes.

Native WSL run:
  https://github.com/emersonbusson/WSL2-Linux-Kernel/actions/runs/37395696464
Build of all thirteen stages with W=1/C=2 on x86_64 and arm64:
  https://github.com/emersonbusson/WSL2-Linux-Kernel/actions/runs/37402766418
The linked mainline run passes all 68 named cases; the WSL leg passes 25.
The final applied source tree is independently pinned in both arch jobs.
The six static CoCo conditions are necessary source checks, not runtime
proof. SEV-SNP, TDX without a paravisor and Arm CCA remain unmeasured.
No platform result is inferred or simulated.

